WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.700
you're going to tell me that I'm not fit to
work in your town?

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.960
Phillip Truesdell and his daughter Hannah
Howe

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.560
run Legacy non-emergency ambulance service in Ohio

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.140
they take patients places for treatment, move people from hospital to hospital

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.100
take people to doctor’s appointments.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.520
I started a business with one truck, and in
a year

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.040
I went to six trucks in the state of Ohio.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:29.180
But when they tried to expand to Kentucky,
just a few minutes away across that river…

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.180
they were told, “you may not pick up patients here”

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.740
I can take them, and I drop them off and I
can't go back and get them.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.200
People waiting hours for medical transportation.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:44.270
Kentucky’s government says a “public health
crisis exists” citing a report that found

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:46.630
“… a shortage of ambulance providers.”

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:50.180
When six-year-old Kyler Truesdell fell of
his motorcycle, his mother says

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.700
The doctor at the local hospital in Maysville told her that
he should be transported to Cincinnati children’s

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.400
to check for internal injuries.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.060
we’d be about a 2 hour
wait before they could get there.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:05.160
Kyler happens to be Hannah’s cousin, but
Kyler still had to wait two hours for an ambulance.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.340
We would’ve done it for free, but it would’ve
been illegal so we couldn’t do it.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.860
It’s illegal because of CON laws.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.940
in Kentucky and 3 other states, you have to
get something called a Certificate of Need

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.460
to run an ambulance service.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:24.860
Certificates are awarded only to businesses
that the state’s bureaucrats deem necessary.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:26.400
So you applied for one?

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.400
We did.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.760
After 11 months of waiting, they discovered
that their application was being protested

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.000
by existing ambulance providers.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.300
we go to court, and these three ambulance
services show up

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.360
They hammered her.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.900
Treated her like she was a criminal.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.240
What do you mean hammered her?

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.110
They just, "Do you know what you're going
to do to this company?...

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:48.250
...Do you know what you're going to do to this
town?"

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:51.520
It wasn't nothing to do with us being physically
able to do it.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.460
Just came through like the big dog not trying
to let anybody else on the porch.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.003
later they learned

00:01:57.003 --> 00:02:00.940
Four ambulance companies applied for permission.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.160
How many got it?

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.660
None.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.420
That didn’t seem right to Phillip

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:09.700
Anybody that draws breath ought to be allowed
to work.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.500
Who gives the big man the right to say, "You
can't work here?"

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.500
The government.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.500
The law.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:16.750
Kentucky ought to change that law.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.360
He and his daughter were lucky to find the
Pacific Legal Foundation, a law firm that

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.980
fights for people’s right to earn a living.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:27.140
46 States operate without certificate of need
laws

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:32.380
Lawyer Anastasia Boden filed the suit with
“The goal … [of restoring] the Constitution’s

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.440
promise of … economic freedom"

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:39.140
"This prevents a proliferation of unnecessary ambulance services."

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:42.720
Traditionally we allow the consumers to decide
what's necessary.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.140
The existing operators are never going to
say that more businesses are necessary because

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:48.720
they don't like the competition.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.360
None of those ambulance providers would talk to us

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.180
But one did send us a statement that says,

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.960
“…saturating a community with more EMS agencies than it can … support ...

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.920
... [leads] all agencies to become watered down."

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.320
"We can't have too much competition because
businesses will start cutting corners."

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:07.520
And that's just absurd.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.420
We now recognize that competition leads to
efficient outcomes.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:14.120
not in states with CON laws, where patients
…wait.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.120
Those are precious minutes to the consumers
who would benefit from ambulances.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:24.260
Not just ambulances, 35 states have con laws
for things like medical imaging companies,

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.160
hospitals, even moving companies.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:31.879
Once you get these laws on the books, it's
very hard to get them off because the monopolies

00:03:31.879 --> 00:03:33.090
like their monopoly.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:38.200
This all started back in the 70s with the
federal government in an attempt to keep prices

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.720
down and to expand service.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.580
But what they found 10 years later was that
certificate of need laws were resulting in

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:46.920
exactly the opposite.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:52.540
The Feds actually learned a lesson from their
mistake and said, "Whoops, this is bad.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:53.740
Let's get rid of these."

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:56.860
But by that time, the damage was done, the
laws were on the books,

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.280
and now it's too hard to get them off.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.420
So you sue.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:01.430
We sue.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:06.870
They’ve taken 14 cases of government overreach
to the supreme court. They won 12.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:11.940
it is an abuse of government power to restrict
somebody's right to earn a living just as

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.300
a handout to the other businesses.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.710
It's not a handout.

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.339
It's protecting a vital service.

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:21.789
It's protecting a vital service for the current
operators only.

00:04:21.789 --> 00:04:24.400
Your local hospital could close!

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.420
In Virginia, when politicians tried to repeal
their state’s hospital CON law,

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:34.770
the established hospitals “… poured more
than $200,000 into [ads] …” like this.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:39.840
the general assembly is voting on legislation
that will financially ruin your local hospital

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.240
We don't see that in other States without
CON laws.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.660
We see hospitals alive and well

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.440
Call your legislator now.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.360
But in Virginia, the ad campaign worked.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.480
The state still has con laws.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:54.889
They like these laws, they keep the competition
out, and they're going to fight to keep these

00:04:54.889 --> 00:04:56.060
laws on the books.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:01.360
People think of hospitals and medical issues
as different from regular competition.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.199
It's just more important.

00:05:03.199 --> 00:05:07.199
If it's more important than it's exactly the
place where we need more competition because

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:13.800
competition has been the driving force of
innovation, lower prices and better services.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:14.900
Everywhere!

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:21.520
Competition works! CON laws are a bad deal
for both consumers and entrepreneurs.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.580
No one should have to ask permission to compete.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:31.200
Who gives them the right to say that you can't
start a business?

